From: OHOS porting run zrepl-0.7.0
Subject: [PATCH] hooks test fixture: finite stdout producer - unbounded
 `while printf` never terminates under OHOS toybox /bin/sh

The fixture's unbounded `while printf ... | head -c $((2<<20))` producer
terminates on POSIX shells only because the writer process itself dies of
SIGPIPE: mainstream /bin/sh (bash/dash/mksh) run printf as a builtin, so
the loop process is the writer and its SIGPIPE death ends the loop once
head exits.

OHOS /bin/sh is toybox and has no printf builtin ("type printf" reports
an alias for the external applet), so every loop iteration forks a fresh
writer process; a single writer's SIGPIPE death does not end the `while :`
loop. The pipeline never reaches EOF, the zrepl hook's cmd.Wait() blocks
forever, and TestHooks/exceed_buffer_limit panicked at the 10m go-test
timeout on the OHOS device (authoritative CI run2: FAIL 600.612s).

Bounded control probes on the same device confirm SIGPIPE/EPIPE delivery
itself is normal for simple pipelines (bash builtin writer loop and
external `seq` writers all terminate same-second once the reader exits):
the deciding variable is whether the writer executes inside the loop
process (shell builtin) or is forked as an external applet. The exact
switch between the two observed hang forms (fork-regeneration spin vs a
writer blocked in write(), the latter TERM/timeout-immune) is not yet
fully characterized and is recorded as an open point.

Replace the producer with a finite `dd` emitting the same 2<<20 bytes.
The Go-side assertions only check the captured buffer length (circular
log capped at 1<<20), not content, so test semantics are preserved on
both POSIX systems and OHOS.
diff --git a/internal/daemon/hooks/test/test-large-stdout.sh b/internal/daemon/hooks/test/test-large-stdout.sh
index fa95b39..49dae3e 100644
--- a/internal/daemon/hooks/test/test-large-stdout.sh
+++ b/internal/daemon/hooks/test/test-large-stdout.sh
@@ -4,9 +4,21 @@
 # The Go test should fail if buffer size exceeds 1MB
 
 pre_testing() {
-	ln=0
-	while :; do printf '%06d: 012345678901234567890123456789012345678901234567890123456789012345678901\n' "$ln"; ln="$(($ln + 1))"; done \
-	| head -c$(( 2 << 20 ))
+	# OHOS port: the original unbounded `while printf` producer terminates
+	# only because the writer process itself dies of SIGPIPE once `head -c`
+	# exits; mainstream /bin/sh run printf as a builtin, so the loop process
+	# is the writer. OHOS /bin/sh (toybox) has no printf builtin, so every
+	# iteration forks an external writer whose SIGPIPE death never ends the
+	# `while :` loop: the pipeline never reaches EOF and the hook's
+	# cmd.Wait() hangs (TestHooks/exceed_buffer_limit panicked at the 10m
+	# go-test timeout). Bounded probes confirm SIGPIPE/EPIPE delivery itself
+	# works for simple pipelines here (bash builtin writer and external seq
+	# writers terminate same-second); the defect is the fork-per-iteration
+	# writer shape. Emit the same 2<<20 bytes from a finite producer
+	# instead; the Go-side assertions only check the captured buffer length
+	# (circular log capped at 1<<20), so test semantics are preserved on
+	# POSIX shells and OHOS alike.
+	dd if=/dev/zero bs=$(( 2 << 20 )) count=1 2>/dev/null
 }
 
 case "$ZREPL_HOOKTYPE" in
